Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-13-2001||11:25 AM||||Anyone mount one on a  notchback yet?  can you post a photo so I know where to bolt it too?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||08-13-2001||12:41 PM||||How did you know what I was thinking?!  Haven't tried it yet, but I always liked the telescoping locking prop rod! My Corvair used the exact same part on its rear hood.  I like them because they are simple, reliable and don't wear out like the pneumatic / hydraulic gizmos they use today (which seem to fail regularly).  Plus, the stiffest wind won't close 'em on you - they lock positively  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">!<P>I think the end brackets would need to change, and don't know if the length is workable, but one of these days I'll look into it.  Unless you beat me to it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!||||reg||10
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-13-2001||04:45 PM|||| grabbed one this past weekend  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   Gonna try to get it figured out this weekend if I get time. I'll let you know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Songman||08-13-2001||07:17 PM||||Someone here recently posted a message about how to adapt a hydraulic trunk (or hood) shock from a Buick to the Fiero engine comparment cover. Do a search for it...<P>Also, Rodney and others sell kits.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||qwikgta||08-14-2001||10:55 PM||||I did the Buick post.  Here is the skinny<P>84 Buick Regal font strut.  <BR>1 40mm bolt (any hardware store)<BR>1 misc bolt (look in your coffee can o' bolts)<BR>2 Nylon (or 4 misc) washers<P>Step 1: Remove the trunk lid attaching bolt on the drivers side that is closest to you.<BR>Step 2: use new 40mm bolt to attach the strut to the trunk lid<BR>Step 3: use misc bolt on the small unused bracket located near the CC vacum can, if no CC then this is easy to get to, it is covered by the heat pad.<P>It is a breeze and you can do it for under $20.00  ( I got a used strut at a yard so I did it for under $1.00<P>Here is the pic.  I also have changed it a little since this pic and added some better nylon spacers instead of wahsers, so now I have $2.00 in to it.<P>Rob<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/strutshot.jpg"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://www.homestead.com/robsfieroproject   <BR>http://www.vafieros.com<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
